# 3. index.js

## Setup (lines 1–16)

| Line | What it does |
|---|---|
| 1 | Finds the `<canvas id="draw">` element. |
| 2 | Gets the 2D drawing context `ctx`, which is the object that has all the drawing commands. |
| 3 | The current color, black to start. |
| 4 | The brush thickness in pixels. Once you choose a size it becomes a string like `"02"`, which the canvas still accepts. |
| 5 | The last known mouse position on the canvas. |
| 6 | Which tool is active: `"free"`, `"line"`, `"rect"`, `"circle"` or `"triangle"`. |
| 7 | Where a shape drag began. `null` means no shape is being drawn. |
| 8 | A saved copy of the canvas pixels, used for the live shape preview. |
| 9 | Paints the color preview box in the header with the starting color. |
| 11–14 | `resize()` sets the canvas to the window width minus 20 (the CSS padding) and the full window height. Setting `width` or `height` **erases the canvas and resets drawing settings** such as color and eraser mode. |
| 16 | Runs it once at load so the canvas has a size. |

## Eraser and tool switching (lines 18–31)

| Line | What it does |
|---|---|
| 18–21 | `erase` first switches to freehand with `setShape("free")`, then sets `globalCompositeOperation = "destination-out"`. That mode makes new strokes **remove** pixels instead of painting. The order matters, because `setShape` resets that mode. |
| 24–25 | `setShape(name)` stores the chosen tool. |
| 26 | Resets drawing to `source-over` (normal painting), which turns the eraser off. |
| 27 | Sets the cursor to a crosshair for shapes. An empty string removes the override, so the pencil cursor from the CSS returns for freehand. |
| 28–30 | Loops over every shape button and adds the `active` class only to the one whose `data-shape` matches. |

