| Line | What it does |
|---|---|
| 34–36 | `startDrawing` runs on `mousedown`. It records the mouse position, and for freehand it stops there. |
| 37 | For a shape, remembers the starting corner. |
| 38 | `getImageData` copies every pixel of the canvas into `snapshot`. |
| 41–44 | `stopDrawing` clears both values when the mouse is released. |
| 47 | `drawShape` updates `pos` to the current mouse position. |
| 48–49 | `dx` and `dy` are how far the mouse has moved from the start. They can be negative, which means dragging left or up. |
| 50–55 | With Shift held (and not for lines), both distances are forced to the larger one, giving a square, circle or evenly proportioned triangle. `dx < 0 ? -size : size` keeps the drag direction. |
| 56–57 | Short names for the start point. |
| 59 | `putImageData` pastes the saved snapshot back, which erases the previous preview. |
| 60 | Starts a new path. |
| 61–63 | A line goes from the start to the current point. |
| 64–65 | `rect(x, y, dx, dy)` draws a rectangle. Negative sizes work. |
| 66–67 | `ellipse` takes a center (the midpoint of the drag box) and two radii (half the width and height). That is why you get ovals unless you hold Shift. |
| 68–72 | The triangle starts at the top center, goes to the bottom right, then the bottom left, and `closePath` joins back to the start. |
| 74–78 | Sets thickness, rounded corners and ends, and the outline and fill color. |
| 79 | If Fill is checked, fills the shape first. Lines are skipped because they have no inside. |
| 80 | Strokes the outline last, so it sits on top of the fill. |

